--- src.orig/chrome/browser/ui/helium/arc_toolbar_host.h
+++ src/chrome/browser/ui/helium/arc_toolbar_host.h
@@ -73,6 +73,66 @@ inline constexpr bool ArcToolbarIsSideba
   return host != ArcToolbarHost::kBand;
 }
 
+// Whether the field lives in the new tab page's centred composer rather than
+// in the band (r8.3, user 2026-08-21).
+//
+// It is the same field either way -- the LocationBarView is reparented, not
+// duplicated -- which is what makes Cmd+L land in the composer for free and
+// what stops the band ever flashing a capsule on the way.
+//
+// ⚠ r8.4 widened this. r8.3 took `band_hosted`, on the reasoning that the
+// hidden address bar mode's sidebar row was its own accepted design and should
+// be left alone. The user overruled that at acceptance, and the reasoning is
+// the module's, not the mode's:
+//
+//   "全隐藏模式下，也是要做成 new tab 的样式……New tab 的逻辑就是打开一个网页中央
+//    带有搜索框的空白页面，这个逻辑跟 app 窗口 UI 布局怎么变没有关系，只要打开
+//    new tab 永远都是网页中央有个搜索框。"
+//
+// So the question is not "which chrome host would have held the field" but
+// "does the shell hold the field at all" -- band or sidebar row, on a blank
+// new tab it moves to the page. `shell_hosts_field` is
+// IsArcBandHostedFor() || IsArcSidebarHostedFor(); the one case that stays
+// false is a window with no Arc shell (popups, horizontal tabs), where there
+// is no composer to move to.
+inline constexpr bool ShouldHostFieldInComposer(bool shell_hosts_field,
+                                                bool blank_new_tab) {
+  return shell_hosts_field && blank_new_tab;
+}
+
+// Whether the band shows its address capsule at all (r8, user 2026-08-21).
+//
+// On a blank new tab it does not, because the field is not there -- it is in
+// the composer. The user's reasoning is the point of the whole module, so it
+// is recorded here rather than lost in a commit message: the composer is where
+// ~90% of typing happens, and a visible capsule at the top of the window
+// competes with it for the same job while being further from the pointer.
+//
+// ⚠ This answers "is the capsule shown", not "where does the toolbar live". It
+// is deliberately NOT a fourth ArcToolbarHost: the extensions, the avatar and
+// the menu stay in the band either way (`Dia:` keeps its own band chrome on
+// the new tab page and simply has no capsule in the middle -- measured
+// 2026-08-21 off the Cmd+T burst). Only the field moves.
+//
+// ⚠ r8.2 had a third parameter, `revealed`, so that Cmd+L could pull the
+// capsule back on a page that had nowhere else to type. The composer is that
+// somewhere else, so it is gone: on a blank new tab the capsule is never shown,
+// full stop.
+//
+// ⚠ This is NOT the complement of ShouldHostFieldInComposer any more (r8.4).
+// That one now fires for the sidebar row too, where this one's `band_hosted`
+// is false and it answers "shown". The caller reconciles them by asking the
+// toolbar whether the field is actually in the composer -- a fact that stays
+// true across the reparent, which neither of these two derived predicates does
+// (see ToolbarView::UpdateArcBandFieldVisibility).
+inline constexpr bool ShouldShowArcBandField(bool band_hosted,
+                                             bool blank_new_tab) {
+  if (!band_hosted) {
+    return true;
+  }
+  return !blank_new_tab;
+}
+
 // Whether the sidebar row's extensions area is shown -- the pinned action
 // icons and the extensions menu button together, since the user reads them as
 // one group and Arc hides the whole group or none of it.
